  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "If all the logarithms are real numbers, the equality\n\n\\[ \\begin{aligned} \\log(x+3)+\\log(x-1)\\\\ =\\log(x^2-2x-3) \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nis satisfied for:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "all real values of \\(x\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "no real values of \\(x\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "all real values of \\(x\\) except \\(x=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "no real values of \\(x\\) except \\(x=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "all real values of \\(x\\) except \\(x=1\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1968-p23.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Combining logarithms would require \\((x+3)(x-1)=x^2-2x-3.\\) This gives \\(x=0.\\) But then \\(x-1=-1,\\) so the logarithm on the left is not real. Hence there are no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Combine the two logarithms on the left",
        "Solve the resulting algebraic equation, then test its domain"
      ],
